>>
endobj
523 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (What are graphemes? Graphemes are letters or combinations of letters that represent a phoneme. There can be more than one grapheme that represents the same phoneme. )
/E (What are graphemes? Graphemes are letters or combinations of letters that represent a phoneme. There can be more than one grapheme that represents the same phoneme. )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
524 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Sound Mounds show graphemes in order of frequency within words. For example, if there is more than one grapheme that represents the phoneme, the graphemes will go in order from most common to least common per position of the grapheme \(beginning, middle, end\). See the example below with the long o. There are more than one way that each phoneme is represented within middle and ending parts, and they are in order based on frequency of the graphemes.   )
/E (Sound Mounds show graphemes in order of frequency within words. For example, if there is more than one grapheme that represents the phoneme, the graphemes will go in order from most common to least common per position of the grapheme \(beginning, middle, end\). See the example below with the long o. There are more than one way that each phoneme is represented within middle and ending parts, and they are in order based on frequency of the graphemes.   )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
525 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Sound Mounds are differentiated to match the progression of phonics skill that would be taught to students starting in Kindergarten and increasing by grade level. The Sound Mounds are easy to differentiate and are helpful to students who are not ready to progress to grade level skills yet.)
/E (Sound Mounds are differentiated to match the progression of phonics skill that would be taught to students starting in Kindergarten and increasing by grade level. The Sound Mounds are easy to differentiate and are helpful to students who are not ready to progress to grade level skills yet.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
526 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (It is important to keep in mind that Sound Mounds represent words and syllables. When using the Sound Mounds, words with two or more syllables can also be encoded using the visual. It is always important to count syllables when starting the process of encoding a word. Even if the word is one syllable, it is important to have students become conditioned to counting syllables to produce fluent readers and writers.)
/E (It is important to keep in mind that Sound Mounds represent words and syllables. When using the Sound Mounds, words with two or more syllables can also be encoded using the visual. It is always important to count syllables when starting the process of encoding a word. Even if the word is one syllable, it is important to have students become conditioned to counting syllables to produce fluent readers and writers.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [5 6]
>>
