551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The star       symbol reprents the patterns that are used in multi-syllable words. You will see this symbol most frequently above open syllable vowels. You can read more about each open syllable in the long vowel section. The graphemes that represents open syllables are typically the most frequent grapheme representing the long vowel in our language in words with more than one syllable. When you are writing words with more than one syllable and that syllable ends with a long vowel, it will more than likely be an open syllable vowel.  )
/E (The star       symbol reprents the patterns that are used in multi-syllable words. You will see this symbol most frequently above open syllable vowels. You can read more about each open syllable in the long vowel section. The graphemes that represents open syllables are typically the most frequent grapheme representing the long vowel in our language in words with more than one syllable. When you are writing words with more than one syllable and that syllable ends with a long vowel, it will more than likely be an open syllable vowel.  )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (For example, the word baby is broken up into two syllables. The syllables are ba/by. When mapping out the word, the star shows students since there is more than one syllable in the word, that is the most common way we see the long a represented within a multi-syllable word.)
/E (For example, the word baby is broken up into two syllables. The syllables are ba/by. When mapping out the word, the star shows students since there is more than one syllable in the word, that is the most common way we see the long a represented within a multi-syllable word.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Beginning Sounds: When teaching with the Sound Mounds, there will be times when there is not beginning sound shown to represent a grapheme. If we look at the /ou/ Sound Mound, we see the ou in the middle of the Sound Mound. )
/E (Beginning Sounds: When teaching with the Sound Mounds, there will be times when there is not beginning sound shown to represent a grapheme. If we look at the /ou/ Sound Mound, we see the ou in the middle of the Sound Mound. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (If we look at the word ouch, we hear the /ou/ at the beginning of the word. Since there is not beginning sound, you would shift to the next sound that you would see on the Sound Mound- this /ou/ would be represented by the ou grapheme. This would only change if the /l/ or /n/ is heard at the end of the word. As always, it is important to map the words and talk about correct spelling. )
/E (If we look at the word ouch, we hear the /ou/ at the beginning of the word. Since there is not beginning sound, you would shift to the next sound that you would see on the Sound Mound- this /ou/ would be represented by the ou grapheme. This would only change if the /l/ or /n/ is heard at the end of the word. As always, it is important to map the words and talk about correct spelling. )
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
