<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Tips)
/E (Tips)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (There are no particular tips when teaching the ea grapheme. The variation of ea comes from the evolution of the English language through the Great Vowel Shift. In Middle English, the ea represented the long e sound to be able to differentiate homophones that contained e-e making the long e sound. Now, it stands as an irregular grapheme producing the short e sound. )
/E (There are no particular tips when teaching the ea grapheme. The variation of ea comes from the evolution of the English language through the Great Vowel Shift. In Middle English, the ea represented the long e sound to be able to differentiate homophones that contained e-e making the long e sound. Now, it stands as an irregular grapheme producing the short e sound. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (You will notice a lot of the words that contain ea end with /d/ or /th/. )
/E (You will notice a lot of the words that contain ea end with /d/ or /th/. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
728 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (The best way to help learn these words is for students to map out the sounds within the words and then map the word. On the Sound Mound cards, there is a heart showing the short ea being an irregular phoneme. Students can heart this part to help with their sound mapping. )
/E (The best way to help learn these words is for students to map out the sounds within the words and then map the word. On the Sound Mound cards, there is a heart showing the short ea being an irregular phoneme. Students can heart this part to help with their sound mapping. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
729 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Also, The short E can sometimes be mistaken for the short I sound. Mouth articulation is important when teaching these sounds. You can use this rhyme to help: /e/ makes me drop my chin, /i/ makes me grin. )
/E (Also, The short E can sometimes be mistaken for the short I sound. Mouth articulation is important when teaching these sounds. You can use this rhyme to help: /e/ makes me drop my chin, /i/ makes me grin. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
730 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Also, the word said can be a trick word within the short e. The ai in said used to be the pronounced with the long a sound, but when the vowel transition happened it was not carried over. The word said can be taught as a heart part. The Sound Mound references this as well. )
/E (Also, the word said can be a trick word within the short e. The ai in said used to be the pronounced with the long a sound, but when the vowel transition happened it was not carried over. The word said can be taught as a heart part. The Sound Mound references this as well. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
