976 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (* The schwa is an unstressed syllable that makes a neutral vowel sound \(or the lazy vowel sound\). * It is the MOST common vowel in the English language. * When a is at the end of a word or syllable, it will produce the schwa sound.   * Y can also make the schwa sound in certain occasions. It is not common, but can happen within some words. \(ex. Pennsylvania, syringe, vinyl, etc.\)  * It is incredibly important to map out sounds within words to identify the schwa sound. The schwa will explain the irregular sounds that are made within words that do not match the normal short vowel sound. HOWEVER, it is important to teach students that the schwa IS a regular, and important, vowel sound in our language. )
/E (* The schwa is an unstressed syllable that makes a neutral vowel sound \(or the lazy vowel sound\). * It is the MOST common vowel in the English language. * When a is at the end of a word or syllable, it will produce the schwa sound.   * Y can also make the schwa sound in certain occasions. It is not common, but can happen within some words. \(ex. Pennsylvania, syringe, vinyl, etc.\)  * It is incredibly important to map out sounds within words to identify the schwa sound. The schwa will explain the irregular sounds that are made within words that do not match the normal short vowel sound. HOWEVER, it is important to teach students that the schwa IS a regular, and important, vowel sound in our language. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
