/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (* Remember, in multi-syllable words consider the placement of all syllables. For example, in the word danger, It is broken up dan/ger. The second syllable is ger, in which the first phoneme we hear is /j/. This would be represented with the g grapheme because the letter that follows is er, which begins with an e. )
/E (* Remember, in multi-syllable words consider the placement of all syllables. For example, in the word danger, It is broken up dan/ger. The second syllable is ger, in which the first phoneme we hear is /j/. This would be represented with the g grapheme because the letter that follows is er, which begins with an e. )
/Pg 59 0 R
