2915 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* There are three /l/ graphemes that represent the /l/ phonemes. These graphemes are -le, -el, and -al, and they all come at the ending of a word.)
/E (* There are three /l/ graphemes that represent the /l/ phonemes. These graphemes are -le, -el, and -al, and they all come at the ending of a word.)
/Pg 66 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2916 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The most common of the three is the grapheme -le. This grapheme typically follows a consonant and consonants that are tall letters or tail letters \(b, d, t, p\). Refer to the list above for examples. When teaching this, it is important that you are teaching at the c+le syllable rule. Every syllable needs a vowel, so if you hear the /l/ and there is no vowel with that syllable, an e will be added. \(ma/ple, hum/ble, lit/tle\) )
/E (The most common of the three is the grapheme -le. This grapheme typically follows a consonant and consonants that are tall letters or tail letters \(b, d, t, p\). Refer to the list above for examples. When teaching this, it is important that you are teaching at the c+le syllable rule. Every syllable needs a vowel, so if you hear the /l/ and there is no vowel with that syllable, an e will be added. \(ma/ple, hum/ble, lit/tle\) )
/Pg 66 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2917 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (When dividing syllables with these words, you can have students go back and count. For example, with the word cattle, have students start at the e. Go back and have them see if there is the L AND a consonant before the vowel. Then they will divde the syllable before the consonant. \(cat/tle\) )
/E (When dividing syllables with these words, you can have students go back and count. For example, with the word cattle, have students start at the e. Go back and have them see if there is the L AND a consonant before the vowel. Then they will divde the syllable before the consonant. \(cat/tle\) )
/Pg 66 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
2918 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The next most common grapheme is -el. We typically use this word when the /l/ sound is alone in the syllable and does not have a consonant following \(nov/el, reb/el\) however, this is not always the case. The el also follows letters that are small letters \(n, m, w, v, r\).  The least common grapheme is -al. These words often are used to describe or show an action. -al can also be used as a suffix, so it is important to talk through the changing of the base word, too. )
/E (The next most common grapheme is -el. We typically use this word when the /l/ sound is alone in the syllable and does not have a consonant following \(nov/el, reb/el\) however, this is not always the case. The el also follows letters that are small letters \(n, m, w, v, r\).  The least common grapheme is -al. These words often are used to describe or show an action. -al can also be used as a suffix, so it is important to talk through the changing of the base word, too. )
/Pg 66 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
