2997 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The /m/ phoneme is known as a nasal sound. This happens because the air flows out from the nose and the vocal cords vibrate to make the sound. This gives this phoneme a nasal sound, which is why when the /m/ follows a short /a/, it produces a whiny sound. This is important to discuss with students when targeting these words. There are extra words in the ending m words so that you can target these with students. )
/E (* The /m/ phoneme is known as a nasal sound. This happens because the air flows out from the nose and the vocal cords vibrate to make the sound. This gives this phoneme a nasal sound, which is why when the /m/ follows a short /a/, it produces a whiny sound. This is important to discuss with students when targeting these words. There are extra words in the ending m words so that you can target these with students. )
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
2998 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The digraph -mb is found only at the ending of words. In Middle English, both letters of this grapheme used to be pronounced and you would hear the b at the ending. However, this changed during the Great Vowel Switch. Within the grapheme -mb, the b is silent. You can teach this as a heart part with students. This grapheme actually originated from Germany. )
/E (* The digraph -mb is found only at the ending of words. In Middle English, both letters of this grapheme used to be pronounced and you would hear the b at the ending. However, this changed during the Great Vowel Switch. Within the grapheme -mb, the b is silent. You can teach this as a heart part with students. This grapheme actually originated from Germany. )
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
2999 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The digraph -mn is also found at the end of words. This is the least frequent way we hear this sound. This grapheme should be taught as a heart part in words. )
/E (* The digraph -mn is also found at the end of words. This is the least frequent way we hear this sound. This grapheme should be taught as a heart part in words. )
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [5]
>>
