<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The /n/ phoneme is known as a nasal sound. This happens because the air flows out from the nose and the vocal cords vibrate to make the sound. This gives this phoneme a nasal sound, which is why when the /n/ follows a short /a/, it produces a whiny sound. This is important to discuss with students when focusing on these words. )
/E (* The /n/ phoneme is known as a nasal sound. This happens because the air flows out from the nose and the vocal cords vibrate to make the sound. This gives this phoneme a nasal sound, which is why when the /n/ follows a short /a/, it produces a whiny sound. This is important to discuss with students when focusing on these words. )
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
3087 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The digraph kn is found only at the beginning of words. In Middle English, both letters of this grapheme used to be pronounced and you would hear the /k/ at the ending. However, this changed during the Great Vowel Switch. Within the grapheme kn, the k is silent. You can teach this as a heart part with students. The kn also has originated from Germany. )
/E (* The digraph kn is found only at the beginning of words. In Middle English, both letters of this grapheme used to be pronounced and you would hear the /k/ at the ending. However, this changed during the Great Vowel Switch. Within the grapheme kn, the k is silent. You can teach this as a heart part with students. The kn also has originated from Germany. )
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
3088 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The digraph gn is also found at the beginning and ending of words. We do not hear the /g/ within this sound. This is the least frequent way we hear this sound. This grapheme should be taught as a heart part in words. This digraph originates from Latin/Greek. )
/E (* The digraph gn is also found at the beginning and ending of words. We do not hear the /g/ within this sound. This is the least frequent way we hear this sound. This grapheme should be taught as a heart part in words. This digraph originates from Latin/Greek. )
/Pg 70 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
