3229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* The grapheme wr is a digraph that is found at the beginning of words. In this grapheme the w is silent in which the /r/ phoneme is produced. Again, like other digraphs, both letters used to be pronounced, but during the Great Vowel Switch this changed. This digraph comes from German origins. There are many homophones when teaching these words \(write/right, ring/wring\) so it is important to teach these words in context. The wr can be a heart part of a word until students grasp the spelling. )
/E (* The grapheme wr is a digraph that is found at the beginning of words. In this grapheme the w is silent in which the /r/ phoneme is produced. Again, like other digraphs, both letters used to be pronounced, but during the Great Vowel Switch this changed. This digraph comes from German origins. There are many homophones when teaching these words \(write/right, ring/wring\) so it is important to teach these words in context. The wr can be a heart part of a word until students grasp the spelling. )
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
