<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* Both of the graphemes s and c can produce the /s/ phoneme. However, the c can only make the /s/ when it is making the soft sound. The letters i, e, and y change the c grapheme from representing the /k/ phoneme to the c representing the /s/ phoneme. We must remember that Sound Mound represent words AND syllables, so when decoding two or more syllable words, we must watch for the pattern. \(Ex. city, can/cel, ci/der\) )
/E (* Both of the graphemes s and c can produce the /s/ phoneme. However, the c can only make the /s/ when it is making the soft sound. The letters i, e, and y change the c grapheme from representing the /k/ phoneme to the c representing the /s/ phoneme. We must remember that Sound Mound represent words AND syllables, so when decoding two or more syllable words, we must watch for the pattern. \(Ex. city, can/cel, ci/der\) )
/Pg 75 0 R
/K [51]
>>
endobj
