3369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* When the grapheme ce is at the end of a word, the c will produce the /s/ phoneme. This is also known as the soft c. The soft c can be found at the beginning and at the ending of words or syllables. We do see the grapheme -ce more often than the grapheme -se. There is no single tip or trick, but when the /s/ is found at the ending of a word and the phoneme /n/ comes before, then it will most likely be the -ce grapheme at the end of the word \(dance, prance, chance\). We also see -ce with nouns \(dice, rice, mice\) more often and -se more frequently with verbs \(erase, chase, close\). However, these rules are interchangable so sound mapping of words is best practice. In some instances the se does sound like the /z/.)
/E (* When the grapheme ce is at the end of a word, the c will produce the /s/ phoneme. This is also known as the soft c. The soft c can be found at the beginning and at the ending of words or syllables. We do see the grapheme -ce more often than the grapheme -se. There is no single tip or trick, but when the /s/ is found at the ending of a word and the phoneme /n/ comes before, then it will most likely be the -ce grapheme at the end of the word \(dance, prance, chance\). We also see -ce with nouns \(dice, rice, mice\) more often and -se more frequently with verbs \(erase, chase, close\). However, these rules are interchangable so sound mapping of words is best practice. In some instances the se does sound like the /z/.)
/Pg 76 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
