3949 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (* As explained in the /m/ and /n/ phoneme pages, when the phonemes /n/ and /m/ follow the short a, the sound becomes distorted. This is because the m and n graphemes are a nasal sound, which changes the sound that the a makes. The a produces a whiny sound instead of the short a. You can use the word list to help teach these sounds, but it is important to explain why this sound is produced. This is important for the /nk/ & /ng/ sounds on the next pages, too. )
/E (* As explained in the /m/ and /n/ phoneme pages, when the phonemes /n/ and /m/ follow the short a, the sound becomes distorted. This is because the m and n graphemes are a nasal sound, which changes the sound that the a makes. The a produces a whiny sound instead of the short a. You can use the word list to help teach these sounds, but it is important to explain why this sound is produced. This is important for the /nk/ & /ng/ sounds on the next pages, too. )
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [28 29 30]
>>
endobj
