4226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The -ed makes the /id/ phoneme when there is a -d or -t at the  ending of the base word \(land, want\). When this happens, it is common that it will sound like there is an additional syllable added to the word \(landed, wanted\). Talk through this and be sure to have students know their base words prior to writing the word.)
/E (The -ed makes the /id/ phoneme when there is a -d or -t at the  ending of the base word \(land, want\). When this happens, it is common that it will sound like there is an additional syllable added to the word \(landed, wanted\). Talk through this and be sure to have students know their base words prior to writing the word.)
/Pg 99 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
4227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The -ed suffix makes the /d/ phoneme when there is a voiced consonant or a vowel at the end of the base word. The voiced consonants included b, d, g, j, l, m, n, ng, r, z, th \(voiced\), v, w, y, and z. These sounds can be felt on the throat when the sound is produced.)
/E (The -ed suffix makes the /d/ phoneme when there is a voiced consonant or a vowel at the end of the base word. The voiced consonants included b, d, g, j, l, m, n, ng, r, z, th \(voiced\), v, w, y, and z. These sounds can be felt on the throat when the sound is produced.)
/Pg 99 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
4228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (The -ed suffix makes the /t/ phoneme when there is a voiceless consonant before the -ed suffix. The voiceless consonants include p, k, f, ch, s, sh, and the \(unvoiced\). The sound can be felt on the throat when the sound is produced to check to see if it is a voiced or voiceless sound. )
/E (The -ed suffix makes the /t/ phoneme when there is a voiceless consonant before the -ed suffix. The voiceless consonants include p, k, f, ch, s, sh, and the \(unvoiced\). The sound can be felt on the throat when the sound is produced to check to see if it is a voiced or voiceless sound. )
/Pg 99 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
